course: books
lesson: 50
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VII, part 2: The Piper at the Gates of Dawn (the music and the island)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: As dawn creeps closer, a breeze stirs the reeds and the Rat suddenly hears far-off piping, music so beautiful that it almost hurts, with a call in it that is «stronger even than the music is sweet».
explain: The Mole hears only the wind in the reeds, but he rows on where the Rat nods, up a backwater towards the great weir; at last the music reaches him too, and he sees tears on his friend's cheeks.
explain: No birds sing, everything is strangely still, and the roses and meadow-sweet look brighter than ever before. The two land on a little island in the arms of the weir, on a lawn ringed by wild fruit trees.
explain: A great awe falls on the Mole, not panic but deep wonder and peace, and when he lifts his eyes he sees the Piper, «the Friend and Helper»: curved horns, kind laughing eyes, pan-pipes in his hand, and little Portly asleep, safe, between his hooves.
explain: «Are you afraid?» whispers the Mole. «Of Him? O, never, never! And yet — and yet — O, Mole, I am afraid!» says the Rat, and they bow their heads in worship. In old Greek stories this figure is Pan, the god of wild places, shepherds and flocks; the book never says his name and tells the scene quietly, as something holy and mysterious.
fact: This is the book's mystical scene: the author tells of a holy, wonderful meeting, as a story, without ever naming the Piper.
fact: The Piper has «curved horns», a «stern, hooked nose», «kindly eyes», a «bearded mouth», «shaggy limbs» and «hooves», and holds «pan-pipes»: this is how the old Greeks pictured Pan, half man and half goat.
fact: Pan-pipes are a row of reed pipes of different lengths tied together and blown across the top.
fact: The book calls him «the Friend and Helper»; his eyes look down on the animals «humourously», and his mouth breaks «into a half-smile».
fact: The Rat hears the music first: «Such music I never dreamed of, and the call in it is stronger even than the music is sweet!»
fact: The Mole at first hears «nothing ... but the wind playing in the reeds and rushes and osiers» (osiers = willows with long thin bendy twigs).
fact: Now the Mole rows and the Rat sits in the stern; «with a slight movement of his head» the Rat directs him into a backwater, a quiet side-branch of the river.
fact: As dawn comes, «no birds sang as they were wont to do» (wont = used to), and «all was marvellously still».
fact: The weir closes the backwater «from bank to bank»; in the middle lies a small island «fringed close with willow and silver birch and alder». The island seems to hide what it holds «behind a veil», keeping it «till the hour should come».
fact: On the island is «a little lawn of a marvellous green», set round with «crab-apple, wild cherry, and sloe».
fact: The Rat whispers: «Here, in this holy place, here if anywhere, surely we shall find Him!»
fact: The Mole feels «a great Awe»: «It was no panic terror — indeed he felt wonderfully at peace and happy»; awe is deep wonder, mixed with a little trembling, before something very great.
fact: The English word «panic» comes from Pan's name: the old Greeks said sudden wild fear in lonely places came from him. The book says carefully that this is NOT panic, but awe.
fact: «Some august Presence was very, very near»: august here means grand and noble (not the month), and a presence is someone you feel is there.
fact: The Mole hardly dares to look up — but the call and the summons still seem to command him, so he raises his eyes.
fact: Portly lies «nestling between his very hooves, sleeping soundly in entire peace and contentment».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «no cap» = honestly; «it's giving» = it feels like; «canny» (North East England) = very; «yeah nah» (Australia, NZ) = no.
exercise: Who hears the far-off piping first? | The Rat, sitting in the stern | The Mole, pulling at the oars | Portly, from far across the water | Otter, waiting by the old ford
exercise: What does the Rat say about the music and the call in it? | The call is stronger even than the music is sweet | The music is sweet, but the call in it is very weak and thin | There is no call in it at all, only a pretty dancing tune | The music is so loud and shrill that it hurts his ears
exercise: What does the Mole hear at first? | Only the wind in the reeds and rushes | The poop-poop of a motor-car, far off down the high road | The field-mice singing their Christmas carol again | Otter barking loudly for joy beside the old ford
exercise: Who is rowing in this part of the chapter? | The Mole | The Rat | Nobody at all | Otter, towing them
exercise: How does the Rat show the Mole the way into the backwater? | With a slight movement of his head | By shouting «Left! Left! Now right!» across the water | By grabbing the oars out of the Mole's paws | By pointing the way with his old cudgel
exercise: What does the Mole see when the music reaches him too? | Tears on the Rat's cheeks | A wide grin on the Rat's face, as at a party | A frown of anger on the Rat's face | The Rat fast asleep in the stern of the boat
exercise: What is strange about the birds at dawn? | None of them sing | They sing louder than ever before | They all fly south together in a long line | They follow the boat up the backwater, chattering
exercise: How do the grass and flowers look that morning? | Fresher and brighter than ever | Grey and dried up after the long hot drought | Covered in white frost, like a winter morning | Trampled flat by the cows from the farm
exercise: What closes the backwater from bank to bank? | The great weir, in a half-circle of foam | A high brick wall built by Toad's gardeners | A great fallen willow lying across the water | A long fishing-net hung by the otters
exercise: What lies in the middle of the stream, in the weir's arms? | A small island | A sunken old boat | The grey mill-house | A sandbank with a green door in it
exercise: Which trees stand round the little lawn on the island? | Crab-apple, wild cherry and sloe | Oak, ash and great old beech | Palm, olive and sweet orange trees | Pine, fir and prickly holly
exercise: What does the Rat whisper on the island? | That here, in this holy place, they will surely find Him | That here they will find Toad's lost money, buried in a box | That here they ought to build a new summer house of their own | That here they should turn round and row straight back home
exercise: What does the Mole feel fall upon him on the island? | A great awe that bows his head, yet leaves him at peace | A sudden panic that sends him running back to the boat | A great hunger, because he has missed his supper | A sleepy wish to lie down on the soft grass at once
exercise: What does «awe» mean in this scene? | Deep wonder, with a little fear, before something great | Anger at being kept out of bed for the whole of a short summer night | A sharp pain in the leg after a long time rowing | Loud laughter at something that is very silly
exercise: What does the Piper hold in his long hand? | Pan-pipes | A shepherd's crook | A silver hunting-horn | A fiddle and its bow
exercise: Which of these is NOT part of the Piper as the Mole sees him? | Wings on his back | Curved horns | Kindly eyes | Shaggy limbs and hooves
exercise: How do the Piper's eyes look down on the animals? | Kindly and humourously | Coldly and very angrily, as at enemies | Sadly, full of tears for the lost child | Closed, as if he were fast asleep
exercise: Where is little Portly? | Asleep between the Piper's hooves | Hiding all alone in a hollow tree on the bank | Swimming in the foam just below the weir | Sitting in the bottom of the Rat's boat
exercise: The Mole asks «Are you afraid?» What does the Rat answer? | «Of Him? O, never, never! And yet — O, Mole, I am afraid!» | «Yes, terribly! Quick, run back to the boat!» | «No, not a bit; nothing in the whole world ever frightens me, Mole!» | «Only of the weir, Mole, not of anything else!»
exercise: What do the two animals do then? | Bow their heads and worship | Run back to the boat and row away | Wake Portly up and take him home at once | Ask the Piper the way back to the ford
exercise: What are pan-pipes? | Reed pipes of different lengths tied in a row | A single long horn made of shining brass | A small round drum with little bells round it | A tin whistle with six holes along the top
exercise: In old Greek stories, who was Pan? | The god of wild places, shepherds and their flocks | The god of the sea, who carried a three-pointed spear | The king of all the gods, who threw lightning bolts | A hero who killed a monster with a magic sword
exercise: The book never says the name «Pan». What does it call him? | The Friend and Helper | The Lord of the River and the Weir | The King of all the Wild Wood | The Old Shepherd of the Downs
exercise: ★★★ Where does the English word «panic» come from? | From Pan: the Greeks said sudden wild fear came from him | From «pan», a cooking pot that bangs very loudly in the kitchen | From the Mole's cry «O my! O my!» said very fast | From «panis», the old Latin word for a loaf of bread
exercise: ★★★ The book says «It was no panic terror». Why is that line clever? | Panic comes from Pan, yet near him the Mole feels awe and peace | The Mole is in fact terribly scared and runs away | Pan never comes near rivers, so there is nothing to fear | Terror is the name of a kind of tree on the island
exercise: Looking back: at the end of chapter I, what did the Mole begin to catch with his ear to the reed-stems? | Something of what the wind went whispering among them | The far-off roar of Toad's new motor-car on the road | The quacking song of the ducks in the backwater | The voice of the Otter telling his story about Toad
exercise: Looking back: in chapter III the Mole felt «the Terror of the Wild Wood». How is the island different? | Then it was fear of enemies; now it is awe with peace | Both times he runs off and hides in a hollow tree | Then he felt happy and safe; now he feels nothing but hatred and anger | There is no difference: he feels the very same thing
test: «Mole heard the music first, no cap.» (no cap = honestly) Put it right. | The Rat heard it first; the Mole heard only wind | They both heard it at the very same moment | Nobody heard any music; it was only a dream | Portly heard it first and called out to them
test: Jack from Newcastle: «the island was canny loud with birds singin'.» (canny = very) Put it right. | There was utter silence in the bird-haunted branches | Yes, every bird on the island was singing its loudest | Only one old owl was hooting in the willows | The ducks round the island were quacking madly
test: Which is TRUE about the scene on the island? | The Mole feels awe, not panic, and is wonderfully at peace | The Piper shouts at the two animals to go away at once | The Rat falls fast asleep on the lawn of marvellous green | Portly is crying bitterly when they first find him there
test: «O, never, never! And yet — and yet — O, Mole, I am afraid!» What does the Rat's answer show? | Love, wonder and trembling, all at once | That he is fibbing to the Mole to look brave | That he wants to go home for his breakfast now | That he is angry with the Piper for hiding Portly
test: Why does the Mole look up, though he hardly dares? | The call and the summons still seem to command him | He hears Otter calling his name from the ford | He only wants to see the sun come up over the weir | The Rat takes his head in both paws and lifts it
test: «It's giving fairy-tale.» (it's giving = it feels like) Which detail makes the island feel like a holy story-place? | It hides what it holds «behind a veil», «till the hour should come» | It has a railway station and a little ticket office | It is full of old tins and rubbish from the boats | It is where Toad keeps all his broken motor-cars, piled up to the roof
test: Zoe: «Pan's a scary monster who stole Portly.» Put it right. | No: the Friend and Helper kept the lost little one safe | Yes: he had shut Portly up in a little wicker cage | Yes: Portly was crying and struggling in his arms | No: the Piper was really Badger dressed up in a costume
test: What do the words «Friend and Helper» tell us about the Piper? | He helps lost and hurt little animals | He fights the weasels with a big sword | He drives the fastest motor-car on the road | He is Toad's rich old uncle from Town
test: Ollie from Sydney: «Mole was rowin', yeah nah, it was Ratty rowin'.» (yeah nah = no) Which of his two words was right? | The first: the Mole was rowing | The second: the Rat was rowing | Neither: Otter was rowing them | Neither: the boat drifted with no oars
test: Looking back: who first told the Mole that nothing is «half so much worth doing as simply messing about in boats»? | The Rat, on the Mole's first day by the river | The Otter, just before he dived after a May-fly | Toad, while rowing his brand-new wager-boat | The Badger, sitting by his big kitchen fire
test: Looking back: which flower «arrived early» in the summer pageant of chapter III, and now fringes the bank? | Purple loosestrife | White snowdrops | The dog-rose | Yellow daffodils
test: Looking back: in chapter V something called the Mole home through his nose. What do that night and this dawn share? | A call that pulls an animal along and must be obeyed | A strong smell of sardines from a cupboard | A letter from Badger saying where to go | The carol of the field-mice in the snow
test: Looking back: Portly's father once came through the snow to Badger's house. What did he say people in a fix usually do? | Go to Badger, or Badger gets to know of it somehow | Go up to Toad Hall and ask Toad for some money | Write a long letter to the police in the town | Hide in a hollow tree until the spring comes
test: Looking back: in which chapter did the Mole first see a river at all? | Chapter I, the day he left his spring-cleaning | Chapter III, on a snowy winter afternoon | Chapter V, near the village with lit windows | Chapter VI, on the day Badger came for Toad
test: ★★★ Count: how many kinds of tree stand round the little lawn itself? (Careful: the island's edge has other trees.) | Three | Two | Four | Six
test: ★★★ «He might not refuse, were Death himself waiting to strike him». What does this mean? | Even if looking meant death, he could not refuse to look | Death is a real animal waiting on the island | The Mole wishes he could die on the island | The Rat is holding one of his pistols ready, in case of weasels
test: ★★★ The Mole is «wonderfully at peace and happy», yet his muscles turn «to water». How can both be true? | Awe is peace and wonder mixed with trembling | One of them must be a mistake by the author | He is happy at heart but ill from the cold night | He is only pretending to be happy for the Rat
exam: Identify the agency by which the Water Rat was first apprised of the proximity of the Piper. | The hearing of a distant piping, imperceptible as yet to his companion | A written communication delivered to him by the elder Otter at the ford | The discovery of a footprint upon the towing-path near the bridge | The intelligence of the field-mice concerning a stranger on the island
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the Mole's state of mind upon the island. | An awe which bowed his head, unaccompanied by panic and attended by peace | A terror of such magnitude that he fled forthwith to the boat | An indifference occasioned by extreme fatigue after a night of rowing | A resentment at having been deprived of his customary rest
exam: Ascertain the situation in which the missing young otter was discovered. | Asleep, in entire peace and contentment, between the hooves of the Friend and Helper | Swimming, in evident distress, in the foam immediately below the weir | Concealed within a hollow tree upon the farther bank of the backwater | Seated alone in a boat moored to a willow beside the old ford
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the two animals' conduct in the Presence? | Crouching to the earth, they bowed their heads and did worship | They addressed the Piper at length concerning the route homeward | They removed the young otter without so much as raising their eyes | They declined to land, remaining in the boat throughout
